<section class="card"><h2>What the data suggests for UNFI</h2>
<div class="recommendation"><strong>Recommendation: Yes—use weather-triggered delivery adjustments, rather than changing every winter delivery schedule.</strong>
<p>Keep normal schedules during manageable conditions, but allow earlier dispatch, wider delivery windows, route changes or temporary rescheduling when forecasts indicate heavy snow, accumulating snow cover or prolonged freezing conditions. Prioritize time-sensitive and temperature-sensitive loads, and coordinate changes with suppliers, carriers and receiving locations.</p></div>
<ul><li><strong>Plan ahead:</strong> Review forecasts 24–48 hours before dispatch and flag routes expected to receive significant snowfall.</li>
<li><strong>Protect service:</strong> Build flexible arrival windows and prioritize essential grocery and agricultural inputs when roads are likely to be hazardous.</li>
<li><strong>Control cost:</strong> Avoid blanket early deliveries or unnecessary schedule changes. Compare extra labor, staging and inventory costs with the cost of late deliveries, missed appointments and weather-related disruption.</li>
<li><strong>Measure results:</strong> Track on-time delivery, miles driven, fuel use, carrier wait time, rescheduling costs and product spoilage before and after weather-triggered changes.</li></ul>
<p class="note"><strong>Important limitation:</strong> This file contains weather observations only. It does not include UNFI shipment records, road conditions, carrier rates, fuel costs, delivery delays or crop volumes. It can identify weather risk periods, but cannot prove that schedule changes will reduce transportation costs. Validate the policy against UNFI's actual route and cost data.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Data source and storage (plain-English notes)</h2>
<p><strong>Where the data comes from:</strong> The uploaded CSV was downloaded from Open-Meteo. It contains hourly weather observations for one coordinate near Minneapolis–Saint Paul (latitude 44.956062, longitude -93.20929; elevation 261 m), in the America/Chicago time zone, for January 1 through December 31, 2025. Fields used: time, temperature at 2 metres (°F), snowfall (inches) and snow depth (feet).</p>
<p><strong>Where the data is stored:</strong> Daily summaries are embedded directly inside this HTML file in the JavaScript variable <code>weatherData</code>. The page does not send the data to a server or need a separate CSV file. The uploaded CSV is the original source; this page uses daily summaries calculated from its hourly records.</p>
<p><strong>How the calculations work:</strong> Daily snowfall is the sum of hourly snowfall. Average daily temperature is the mean of hourly temperatures. Maximum snow depth is the largest hourly snow-depth reading that day. Freezing hours counts hourly readings at or below 32°F.</p>
<p><strong>Filter limitation:</strong> The source file covers one location and one year, and has no county or crop field. The year selector therefore contains only 2025; the month selector provides a useful within-year filter. County- or crop-level comparisons require data with those fields or weather data for additional locations.</p>
<p class="source">Source file: open-meteo-44.96N93.21W261m.csv · Data period: 2025 · Units are shown beside each measure.</p></section></main>
